/* Sources-read data for directing. Written by _template/kit.py sources from docs/course-directing-notes.md and the read records in sources/. Edit those, not this file. */
(window.COURSE_SOURCES = window.COURSE_SOURCES || {})["directing"] = "# Directing for stage and screen: source notes\n\nBuilt 11/10/2026 for an intermediate learner, covering stage first and screen second. Prices and rules in the sources are American unless stated.\n\n**Read in full:**\n- *Directing* (Nick Garcia, LibreTexts, CC BY 4.0, 2020): all nine chapters (what is a director, preparation, breaking down the script, design meetings, budgeting, casting, lessons in staging, the rehearsal process, the actor's process), as fetched from LibreTexts.\n- *Contemporary World Cinema* (eCampusOntario Pressbooks, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0): the chapters on Mise-en-sc\u00e8ne, Cinematography, Editing and Acting.\n- *An Actor Prepares* (Konstantin Stanislavski, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood, 1936): read in full from a copy of the text. The course cites the book, not the copy. The chapters used closely were Action, Units and Objectives, Emotion Memory, Communion, Adaptation, The Unbroken Line and The Super-Objective. The Imagination, Concentration, Relaxation, Faith and a Sense of Truth, Inner Motive Forces, Inner Creative State and Threshold of the Subconscious chapters were skimmed for context and are not cited.\n- Britannica entries for Stanislavsky, Granville-Barker (search excerpt plus the print version), Eisenstein and Brook, fetched in full for the first, third and fourth.\n\n**Read in part:**\n- *Prefaces to Shakespeare*, volume VI (Batsford, 1974): Edward Moore's introduction and passages of the prefaces on *The Winter's Tale*, *Twelfth Night* and *A Midsummer Night's Dream* were read from the scanned text; the Macbeth preface, other prefaces and volumes I to V were not read. The scan has OCR errors.\n- Britannica on Meyerhold and on biomechanics, Encyclopedia.com on Meyerhold, media-studies.com on Eisenstein's five methods, the RSC page on Brook's 1970 Dream and the Wikipedia page on *The Empty Space*: read as search excerpts, not fetched whole.\n\n**Not read:**\n- Meyerhold's own writings and Edward Braun's *Meyerhold on Theatre*. The terms otkaz, posyl and tochka come from a student study site and are marked unverified.\n- Eisenstein's *Film Form*. The scan we found had no text layer. His five methods are taken from a secondary account.\n- Brook's *The Empty Space* (the book). We use the Wikipedia article for the four modes and the definition of an empty space.\n- Stanislavski's *Building a Character*, *Creating a Role* and *My Life in Art*. Tempo-rhythm and the late method of physical actions are not taught.\n- The *Contemporary World Cinema* chapters on sound and genre; the video clips embedded in the book were not watched.\n- The Harvard thesis on Granville-Barker's Savoy productions (listed as a search result only).\n- UK and Irish law and guidance on performance rights, licensing, fire safety, safeguarding and performer safety.\n\n**Source disagreements and cautions:**\n- The film textbook calls Stanislavski's approach emotion-first and says actors let go of the text; *An Actor Prepares* tells the actor to attend to the given circumstances and forget feelings. The course shows them side by side and says which it follows. Britannica adds that Lee Strasberg's American Method is an adaptation and that Stanislavski kept changing his approach.\n- Garcia's gender examples rely on stereotypes by his own admission, and his claim that over 70 percent of communication is non-verbal has no source. The course warns about both.\n- Garcia's distance guide (feet between actors) is one teacher's rule of thumb. Distances vary by culture.\n- The course's Britannica entries disagree about when biomechanics began (Hedda Gabler, 1906, or the early 1920s). The course says which it treats as which.\n- Britannica criticises Eisenstein's *Strike* for imposing a symbol that falsified the scene.\n- *An Actor Prepares* is a teaching narrative with an invented class and teacher, not a record of real rehearsals.\n\n**Limits:** Garcia's budget figures, rights fees and process are for American educational and community theatre in 2020. All worked examples (the waiting-room scene, the budget, coverage times) are invented and marked illustrative. The mappings between stage and screen devices are our inference and are labelled. This is not legal or safety advice, and fights, intimate scenes and risky movement need qualified supervision.\n\n## Book supplied by the learner (added 19/10/2026)\n\n**The Art of Alfred Hitchcock, Donald Spoto, second revised edition, Anchor Books, 1992 (Knopf Doubleday ebook, 2012)** (46 chapters, about 96,000 words). The review is written up in `source-review-hitchcock.md`.\n- Read in full: Chapter 20 Rope, Chapter 23 Strangers on a Train, Chapter 26 Rear Window, Chapter 33 Psycho, and the introduction to the centenary edition.\n- Read in full on 19/10/2026 (45 of 46 parts; only the contents list was skipped). Review: `source-review-hitchcock.md`, Status: complete.\n- Added to the course: planting, bookends and the controlling image (module 1), evidence from cast and crew on directing actors (module 2), the cost of long takes and lost preparation (module 3), and a worked model for designing a sequence (module 5). Spoto agrees with the earlier long-take case study, so nothing there was removed.\n- Limits: Spoto is one critic's reading, and several points rest on Hitchcock's remarks as Spoto reports them. The accounts of Hitchcock and actors come from a critic and one leading actor, not from rehearsal records. Facts about the films (running of takes, camera tricks) are Spoto's account and were not checked against other sources.\n";
(window.COURSE_SOURCEMAP = window.COURSE_SOURCEMAP || {})["directing"] = {"modules": {"1": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "2": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "3": "Working with actors II: communion, the through-line and the rehearsal room", "4": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "5": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "6": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "cap": "Direct a three-minute scene on stage and on camera, and review both"}, "title": "Directing for stage and screen: source notes", "intro": "Built 11/10/2026 for an intermediate learner, covering stage first and screen second. Prices and rules in the sources are American unless stated. **Read in full:** - *Directing* (Nick Garcia, LibreTexts, CC BY 4.0, 2020): all nine chapters (what is a director, preparation, breaking down the script, design meetings, budgeting, casting, lessons in staging, the rehearsal process, the actor's process), as fetched from LibreTexts. - *Contemporary World Cinema* (eCampusOntario Pressbooks, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0): the chapters on Mise-en-scène, Cinematography, Editing and Acting. - *An Actor Prepares* (Konstantin Stanislavski, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood, 1936): read in full from a copy of the text. The course cites the book, not the copy. The chapters used closely were Action, Units and Objectives, Emotion Memory, Communion, Adaptation, The Unbroken Line and The Super-Objective. The Imagination, Concentration, Relaxation, Faith and a Sense of Truth, Inner Motive Forces, Inner Creative State and Threshold of the Subconscious chapters were skimmed for context and are not cited. - Britannica entries for Stanislavsky, Granville-Barker (search excerpt plus the print version), Eisenstein and Brook, fetched in full for the first, third and fourth. **Read in part:** - *Prefaces to Shakespeare*, volume VI (Batsford, 1974): Edward Moore's introduction and passages of the prefaces on *The Winter's Tale*, *Twelfth Night* and *A Midsummer Night's Dream* were read from the scanned text; the Macbeth preface, other prefaces and volumes I to V were not read. The scan has OCR errors. - Britannica on Meyerhold and on biomechanics, Encyclopedia.com on Meyerhold, media-studies.com on Eisenstein's five methods, the RSC page on Brook's 1970 Dream and the Wikipedia page on *The Empty Space*: read as search excerpts, not fetched whole. **Not read:** - Meyerhold's own writings and Edward Braun's *Meyerhold on Theatre*. The terms otkaz, posyl and tochka come from a student study site and are marked unverified. - Eisenstein's *Film Form*. The scan we found had no text layer. His five methods are taken from a secondary account. - Brook's *The Empty Space* (the book). We use the Wikipedia article for the four modes and the definition of an empty space. - Stanislavski's *Building a Character*, *Creating a Role* and *My Life in Art*. Tempo-rhythm and the late method of physical actions are not taught. - The *Contemporary World Cinema* chapters on sound and genre; the video clips embedded in the book were not watched. - The Harvard thesis on Granville-Barker's Savoy productions (listed as a search result only). - UK and Irish law and guidance on performance rights, licensing, fire safety, safeguarding and performer safety. **Source disagreements and cautions:** - The film textbook calls Stanislavski's approach emotion-first and says actors let go of the text; *An Actor Prepares* tells the actor to attend to the given circumstances and forget feelings. The course shows them side by side and says which it follows. Britannica adds that Lee Strasberg's American Method is an adaptation and that Stanislavski kept changing his approach. - Garcia's gender examples rely on stereotypes by his own admission, and his claim that over 70 percent of communication is non-verbal has no source. The course warns about both. - Garcia's distance guide (feet between actors) is one teacher's rule of thumb. Distances vary by culture. - The course's Britannica entries disagree about when biomechanics began (Hedda Gabler, 1906, or the early 1920s). The course says which it treats as which. - Britannica criticises Eisenstein's *Strike* for imposing a symbol that falsified the scene. - *An Actor Prepares* is a teaching narrative with an invented class and teacher, not a record of real rehearsals. **Limits:** Garcia's budget figures, rights fees and process are for American educational and community theatre in 2020. All worked examples (the waiting-room scene, the budget, coverage times) are invented and marked illustrative. The mappings between stage and screen devices are our inference and are labelled. This is not legal or safety advice, and fights, intimate scenes and risky movement need qualified supervision.", "standard": false, "books": [{"key": "craig-1912", "name": "On the Art of the Theatre", "by": "Craig, Edward Gordon, 1872-1966", "words": 76188, "status": "full", "read": 20, "skipped": [], "sections": 20, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 6, "verdicts": {"note": 3, "contradicted": 1, "extended": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"found_by": "user", "added": "2026-10-25", "source_file": "Craig - On the Art of the Theatre (1912).pdf", "note": "Date is when the file arrived in the source ingest folder."}, "states": {"open": 6}, "uses": [], "origin": "user", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "hitchcock", "name": "The Art of Alfred Hitchcock", "by": "Donald Spoto", "words": 96435, "status": "full", "read": 45, "skipped": [{"reason": "contents list only", "titles": ["CONTENTS"]}], "sections": 46, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 40, "verdicts": {"extended": 30, "confirmed": 2, "note": 7, "contradicted": 1}, "review": "complete", "modules": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], "note": "", "prov": {"found_by": "user", "added": "2026-10-19", "source_file": "The Art of Alfred Hitchcock.epub", "note": "Date is when the file arrived in the source ingest folder."}, "states": {"applied": 36, "confirmed": 2, "skipped": 2}, "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: turning the idea into a look", "chapters": ["Chapter 23", "Chapter 33"], "points": ["Donald Spoto's study of Hitchcock shows a director turning a theme into a look. For Strangers on a Train, Hitchcock added to the novel a system of doubles and crossings: two tennis games, two drinks ordered as doubles, two fathers, two fairground trips, the lighter with crossed racquets, and an opening that shows only two pairs of shoes meeting.", "Spoto says the form of the film is the direct clue to its meaning.", "In Psycho the repeated idea is the split. Mirrors appear throughout, and the frame itself is cut: a crane bisects the horizon, a telephone pole bisects Marion's car, and the horizontal motel stands against the vertical house.", "Spoto lists the doubles, crossings, tennis, lighter and dark backgrounds as changes or additions."]}, {"title": "Planting, bookends and the controlling image", "chapters": ["Chapter 29", "Chapter 3", "Chapter 7", "Chapter 15", "Chapter 31", "Chapter 38", "Chapter 37"], "points": ["Spoto's study of Hitchcock keeps returning to a few moves that turn a theme into a plan. The moves can be borrowed for stage or screen.", "Vertigo is Spoto's largest example. Everything grows from the spiral: the title design, the stair, the hair, the car routes and the camera that circles. The camera pans right to left in the first half and left to right in the second, and red and green recur. Colour pairs were planned in the shooting script and notes.", "Topaz shows the same method on a larger map: yellow marks French characters, red marks Communism, and lavender marks tenderness and death, set out in production files with the designer.", "Young and Innocent adds a motif to its bookends. Eyes, glasses and a blindfold carry the film's idea of seeing and judging.", "Its long crane move from a ballroom to the murderer's twitching eyes works because it ties us to a clue, which is Spoto's point that content should determine technique."]}]}, {"module": 2, "title": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: acting for the camera", "chapters": ["Chapter 17", "Foreword", "Chapter 39", "Chapter 11", "Chapter 5", "Chapter 32", "Chapter 35"], "points": ["Hitchcock cultivated the remark that actors should be treated like cattle. Spoto reads it as puckish humour, and says the real target of Hitchcock's resentment was inflated pay for unworthy performers, while in practice he was very much an actor's director.", "Grace Kelly, who worked with him, wrote that he has a special way with actors: his humour puts them at ease and his patience gives them confidence, although sometimes he simply wears them down until he gets what he wants.", "Spoto also points to the scale of screen acting he admired in Ingrid Bergman's Spellbound: the way she turns her head, folds her hands and stifles a sob.", "More evidence comes from the cast and crew of Family Plot, interviewed by Spoto. Bruce Dern said the director explained what each shot was supposed to say and what the actor had to do, then left room for freshness; an image of William Powell kept William Devane light, and he 'played the clothes'. Karen Black said everything was worked out before the set day.", "Of Spellbound, Spoto says Hitchcock never over-directed and altered image size and background to suit the emotion of a shot. For a refugee actor who knew no English, he summarised each scene just before the take."]}]}, {"module": 3, "title": "Working with actors II: communion, the through-line and the rehearsal room", "lessons": [{"title": "The through-line and the super-objective", "chapters": ["Chapter 4", "Chapter 15", "Chapter 28"], "points": ["Spoto's readings of Hitchcock show a through-line built from pairs and repeats. In The 39 Steps one theme, trust and betrayal against appearance and reality, governs every sequence. Three couples illuminate it (Hannay and Pamela, the crofter and his wife, the innkeeper and his wife), a gesture of offering supper recurs, and the film begins and ends in a theatre.", "Shadow of a Doubt opens with a paired sequence of two cities, two houses and two bedrooms, with two characters lying on beds, so the theme is stated before any dialogue. A long list of doubles and echoes then ties the two Charlies together.", "Spoto also credits Teresa Wright's performance to acute listening, and notes that shooting out of sequence asks an actor to hold the arc of the part.", "Tone is part of the line too. Spoto reads The Trouble with Harry as played on understatement: the characters treat a corpse matter-of-factly, in bright autumn rather than night. He also warns critics against reading too many symbols into a work."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: rehearsing and directing on a film set", "chapters": ["Chapter 37", "Chapter 39", "Chapter 20", "Chapter 21"], "points": ["Calm preparation is the point. On Topaz the script was written only days ahead of shooting, against Hitchcock's habit of working out every element in advance, and the screenwriter said more time would have helped.", "Family Plot's cinematographer said the director never moved the camera without a reason.", "Hitchcock's Rope was planned entirely in takes of about ten minutes, the length of time a magazine of film could run in the camera.", "To do it, the camera moved through the apartment while walls were lifted silently into the flies, grips removed and replaced furniture just out of shot, and actors hit marks on the floor for each line. At the end of a take the camera moved close to a dark object, such as the back of a jacket, so the reel could be changed without a visible cut.", "A mistake meant starting the whole take again, so every camera movement and position had to be arranged exactly in advance."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: blocking for a frame", "chapters": ["Chapter 18", "Chapter 23", "Chapter 10", "Chapter 13", "Chapter 16", "Chapter 30"], "points": ["In the balcony scene of Notorious, Devlin and Alicia talk and kiss as they move from the terrace through the living room to the hall telephone and the door, in a single take with the camera staying close to them. Spoto reads the scene as more than skilful camerawork: it is also a plain picture of Devlin's character, who can manage only a chilly lovemaking.", "Strangers on a Train uses a cut where a stage would use blocking. Twice Guy checks his watch and there is a matched cut to Bruno making the same gesture, and when Bruno asks about sunset the film cuts to Guy turning to look at the setting sun.", "In Rebecca the heroine is held to the extreme right of the frame in the first hotel scenes, almost pushed out of view by the overbearing Mrs Van Hopper and the glamorous Olivier. Where she stands in the frame shows how little power she has.", "A long shot of Mrs Van Hopper looking the girl up and down is repeated almost exactly with Mrs Danvers, so the same glance links the two dominating women.", "Suspicion sketches Lina in three shots: a tiny figure before a window in a formal room, a medium close-up turning magazine pages, then a close-up of her face."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "Continuity: master shot, coverage and the line", "chapters": ["Chapter 23"], "points": ["A later example is Strangers on a Train, where Hitchcock cuts between a bright tennis match and a dark drain, a crosscut Spoto calls famous."]}, {"title": "Case studies: restricted viewpoint, the long take and the cut", "chapters": ["Chapter 20", "Chapter 26", "Chapter 21", "Chapter 33", "Chapter 1", "Chapter 3", "Chapter 6", "Chapter 19"], "points": ["Rear Window limits the viewpoint to what can be seen from one apartment window. Spoto says the film is the most triumphant of Hitchcock's experiments with a restricted viewpoint, after Lifeboat and Rope.", "Where many of his films open by moving from outside to inside, this one moves from inside to outside, so what we see is what Jeff looks at and his viewpoint limits ours.", "Hitchcock told Spoto that the film worked because it is the epitome of the subjective treatment: a man looks, he sees, and he reacts, which builds a mental process out of pictures.", "That is the pattern of look, object, reaction that Kuleshov described in the last lesson. The most chilling moment, in Spoto's reading, is when Thorwald looks from the wedding ring on Lisa's finger across to Jeff, and so straight out at us.", "Spoto notes that shots in films normally last about five to fifteen seconds, and that directors, writers and editors use the cut to vary the viewpoint, hold interest, change setting and build tension."]}, {"title": "Designing a sequence: a worked model", "chapters": ["Chapter 29", "Chapter 34", "Storyboard", "Chapter 25", "Chapter 30", "Chapter 24", "Chapter 2", "Chapter 31"], "points": ["Spoto describes the Albert Hall sequence of The Man Who Knew Too Much (1956) as wordless, about twelve minutes and 124 shots. It makes a good model for how to design a sequence.", "For The Birds, Hitchcock pinned long sheets to his office walls graphing the rise and fall of each sequence and the alternation of dialogue with action, and he tied each attack to the emotional line before it. The film runs to over fourteen hundred shots.", "A storyboard is drawn from the final shooting script and serves as a guide for director and cinematographer, though it is often changed on the day.", "Dial M for Murder stretches a few seconds of real time by crosscutting the killer's wait, the watch and the telephone's mechanism, so the audience grows impatient on the wrong side.", "The Wrong Man avoids eye level and uses high and low angles so the world feels off-centre as Manny's trouble grows; a broken mirror splits his image."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "Designers, budget and the production meeting", "chapters": ["Chapter 14", "Chapter 37"], "points": ["Spoto's chapter on Saboteur shows a budget trade-off on a film. The producer had spent heavily on script revisions, and Hitchcock had insisted on many expensive setups and a few lavish sets, so economy came later in cheap background shots, painted backdrops, miniatures and rear projection, and in cheaper leads than he wanted.", "Topaz shows the opposite risk. A film that Hitchcock normally had fully planned was shot while the script was being written a few days ahead of each scene, and the screenwriter said more time would have made it better."]}]}], "origin": "user", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Planting, bookends and the controlling image", "n": 6}, {"text": "module 5, lesson Case studies, Earlier tools", "n": 4}, {"text": "module 4, lesson Screen lens: blocking for a frame", "n": 4}, {"text": "auto: module 5, lesson Designing a sequence: a worked model", "n": 4}, {"text": "module 3, lesson The through-line, A through-line you can see on screen", "n": 3}, {"text": "module 2, lesson Screen lens: acting for the camera", "n": 3}, {"text": "module 1, lesson Planting, bookends and the controlling image", "n": 2}, {"text": "auto: module 2, lesson Screen lens: acting for the camera", "n": 2}, {"text": "module 6, lesson Designers, What preparation spending buys", "n": 2}, {"text": "module 5, lesson Designing a sequence (Blackmail sound)", "n": 1}], "skipped": [{"text": "Stage Fright: illustrative only, no lesson change needed", "n": 1}, {"text": "Filmography: credits only, no lesson change needed", "n": 1}]}}, {"key": "katz-shot-by-shot", "name": "Film Directing Shot by Shot", "by": "Steven D. Katz", "words": 90535, "status": "full", "read": 15, "skipped": [], "sections": 15, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 11, "verdicts": {"confirmed": 1, "extended": 8, "note": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"found_by": "user", "added": "2026-10-25", "source_file": "Film Directing Shot by Shot - 25th Anniversary Edition.pdf", "note": "Date is when the file arrived in the source ingest folder."}, "states": {"confirmed": 1, "open": 10}, "uses": [], "origin": "user", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "niculescu-2019", "name": "Defining the stage director and teaching theater directing", "by": "", "words": 2894, "status": "full", "read": 1, "skipped": [], "sections": 1, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 5, "verdicts": {"confirmed": 1, "note": 2, "extended": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"found_by": "user", "added": "2026-10-25", "source_file": "balardim,+Irina+Niculescu+-+EN.pdf", "note": "Date is when the file arrived in the source ingest folder."}, "states": {"confirmed": 1, "open": 4}, "uses": [], "origin": "user", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "norrthon-2023", "name": "Cueing in Theatre: Timing and Temporal Variance in Rehearsals of Scene Transitions", "by": "Stefan Norrthon ", "words": 9453, "status": "full", "read": 2, "skipped": [], "sections": 2, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 4, "verdicts": {"extended": 2, "note": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"found_by": "user", "added": "2026-10-25", "source_file": "s10746-023-09672-8.pdf", "note": "Date is when the file arrived in the source ingest folder."}, "states": {"open": 4}, "uses": [], "origin": "user", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [], "skipped": []}}], "pages": [{"key": "cwc", "status": "unknown", "name": "Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting", "by": "eCampusOntario Pressbooks, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "modules": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: turning the idea into a look", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["On screen, nothing in the picture is accidental, and everything is planned to serve the story.", "Mise-en-scène means the overall look and feel, built from setting, character, lighting and composition.", "The same holds on a film set, where the production designer turns your look into the work of several departments.", "Low-key lighting is high contrast with hard shadows, and high-key is flatter and brighter.", "The look can mark a genre or a movement. German Expressionism used exaggerated sets and heavy shadow; Italian neorealism rarely built sets and often used non-professional actors."]}]}, {"module": 2, "title": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "lessons": [{"title": "Given circumstances and the magic 'if'", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["The popular 'Method' and Stanislavski's own books are not the same thing."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: acting for the camera", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["Acting began on the stage, and the first screen actors carried the stage habit of playing big for the back row.", "The camera was far more intimate than anyone expected, so the style slowly shrank to suit it.", "The textbook also says that the theatrical style of early films worked for the audiences of the time, because they knew nothing else.", "The film textbook says the camera, not the other actor, is the most important relationship in a scene for a screen performer.", "It points to a two-minute close-up of Nicole Kidman in Birth as a study in subtlety that would not work from the balcony."]}]}, {"module": 3, "title": "Working with actors II: communion, the through-line and the rehearsal room", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: rehearsing and directing on a film set", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["A film is a collaboration with a thousand moving parts.", "The actor controls the performance from the start of a take to the end, but the editor shapes it afterwards.", "The director holds the shape of the whole film so the actor can focus on the scene in front of them.", "The director knows how every piece will contribute to the whole, which lets an actor trust a direction that seems strange in the moment.", "A director may favour a particular way of interacting across a whole body of work, such as eye contact or touch."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "Screen lens: blocking for a frame", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["A film frame is a picture that the director chooses, and the audience cannot look outside it.", "Composition means arranging people, objects and setting inside the frame.", "It can bring balance or imbalance, reveal or hide information and show power, all before anyone speaks.", "The textbook describes the rule of thirds: divide the frame into thirds and place interest at the crossing points.", "It shows that two characters of unequal size in the frame suggest unequal power."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "The shot: size, lens and movement", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["A shot is a decision about what the audience sees and how it feels.", "The film textbook says a close-up creates a feeling of intimacy, and an extreme long shot suggests disconnection and emotional distance.", "Directors and cinematographers use a shorthand for how close or far a subject appears.", "Focal length decides the angle of view and the magnification.", "A shorter focal length gives a wider view, and a longer one gives a narrower view with more magnification."]}, {"title": "Continuity: master shot, coverage and the line", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["The goal of continuity editing is a smooth flow of images and sound that keeps the viewer oriented in space and time.", "It aims to be unobtrusive, so the viewer is drawn into the experience and not reminded they are watching a film.", "Its main tools are cutting on action, match cuts, screen direction and the master shot with coverage.", "The textbook shows the idea with a café scene. The editor starts on the master, cuts to an over-the-shoulder on each speaker, moves to close-ups as tension builds, and returns to the master to reset after a moment of conflict.", "Coverage also lets the editor cut around a poor take or condense a scene."]}, {"title": "Rhythm, the Kuleshov effect and discontinuity", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["Lev Kuleshov showed in 1918 that the audience reads meaning from the order of shots.", "He cut the same shot of a famous actor's face next to soup, a child in a coffin and a woman, and audiences praised his different expressions.", "The face was the same each time.", "The viewers supplied the feeling because of what came next to it.", "An edit can skip time with an ellipsis, such as a car arriving and a cut to a woman at the door."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Sergei Eisenstein", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["The film textbook says the Odessa Steps was built to create an emotional effect and not to give clear geography: it cuts violence against innocence, repeats images and holds some while flashing others. Battleship Potemkin (1925) was commissioned to mark the 1905 revolution.", "It was built to create an emotional effect and not to give clear geography."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The space and the audience", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["The film textbook says a production can film in a real place or build a set on a soundstage.", "A soundstage is a sound-proof building that gives full control of light and sound.", "That control matters for a series that returns to the same sets for months or years.", "A soundstage buys control; a location costs less to build."]}, {"title": "Tech, dress rehearsal, opening and review", "chapters": ["Contemporary World Cinema, chapters on mise-en-scène, cinematography, editing and acting"], "points": ["On screen the handover is different. When the cameras stop, the actor's job is done and the editor sifts the takes.", "The director still works with the editor, but a film is a collaboration of a thousand moving parts.", "On screen, plan time with the editor before wrap."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "garcia", "status": "unknown", "name": "Directing", "by": "Nick Garcia, LibreTexts, CC BY 4.0, 2020", "modules": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, "cap"], "note": "Garcia, open textbook, chapters 1 to 9", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "What a director does, and the order of the work", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Ask ten directors what directing is and you will hear ten answers.", "Each director builds a system from the people and projects they have worked with.", "Garcia still finds common ground between them.", "A director is a leader and the central point of communication between everyone making the production.", "A director needs an idea of what they want and the openness to let artists shape it."]}, {"title": "Reading the play: scenes, French scenes and the story shape", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Your first job is to read the play, and then read it again.", "Garcia says that when a show is chosen for you, you read it until you find how you connect to its themes.", "The first read also gives you pictures in your head, which are only one interpretation among many.", "A scene is a section the playwright has marked.", "In a scene each character struggles for what they want, until one makes a choice that succeeds or complicates things."]}, {"title": "Finding the theme and knowing your audience", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia calls the theme the heartbeat of the story and the message the audience takes away.", "The best themes let the audience feel they worked it out themselves, because preaching makes them disengage.", "He suggests a theme that is short, simple and strong, like the motto of your production.", "Which line keeps coming back to me?", "Garcia says the true theme sometimes emerges in rehearsal, so hold it with an open hand."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: turning the idea into a look", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["It is the screen version of Garcia's advice to give your team a shared picture.", "Garcia says to bring visual aids to each designer, because the clearer your pictures, the more focused they can be.", "Without direction, designers will decide for themselves, and you will frustrate them if you change course later.", "Give each designer pictures, not only words."]}]}, {"module": 2, "title": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "lessons": [{"title": "Given circumstances and the magic 'if'", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia turns this into four questions an actor can answer on paper: who, what, when and where.", "Garcia suggests going through these in front of the whole company, so every actor and designer works in the same world.", "Garcia lists who, what, when and where."]}, {"title": "Objectives, units and verbs", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia's version is the scene objective: what the character needs to get in order to leave the scene.", "Garcia asks the actor to name three to five dominant tactics, the 'go to' ways the character gets others to do what they want.", "He lists a strongest, a next strongest and a weakest way.", "Start with physical objectives and add tactics when stuck."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: acting for the camera", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia says that in a large proscenium house the acting must be exaggerated to fill the space, or it looks flat.", "Size follows space and lens; the objective stays."]}]}, {"module": 3, "title": "Working with actors II: communion, the through-line and the rehearsal room", "lessons": [{"title": "Communion and adaptation: playing to each other", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Adaptations also change with the partner, because people alter their behaviour according to who is in the room."]}, {"title": "The through-line and the super-objective", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Through-line in one sentence (Garcia's form): she controls the order in which she learns things."]}, {"title": "The rehearsal room: a week, a note, a breath", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia breaks the first week into introductions and design presentations, team building, character work, then script work through Act 1 and Act 2.", "The aim is a shared reading of each scene's given circumstances, objectives and function.", "The atmosphere you set in week one becomes the culture of the rehearsal.", "Garcia says people need to know what they do well more than what to fix.", "They can work on only one improvement at a time, so choose with care."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: rehearsing and directing on a film set", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The stage, its focus and the actor's body", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia says playwrights tell the story through dialogue, designers through colour and texture, actors through emotion, and the director through movement and stage pictures.", "Stage training is silent at first, because most storytelling is non-verbal and that is your contribution.", "He calls staging the director's gym.", "Directions are from the actor's point of view, facing the audience.", "Upstage is away from the audience and downstage is toward it."]}, {"title": "Levels, distance, crosses and the stage picture", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia's first rule of composition is the visual triangle.", "Three or more actors in a straight line make a flat picture, while one stepping up or downstage makes a triangle with a point of focus.", "Height works the same way: if two sit and one stands, the standing figure is the apex and holds focus.", "Garcia gives rules of thumb for the distance between two actors.", "Over ten feet usually reads as strangers or as a barrier."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: blocking for a frame", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "Theatre lens: transitions and loops as stage edits", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["A stage has no cut, but it has transitions. Garcia defines a transition as the way the audience moves from one scene or location to another.", "Many are done with a lighting change and a set move, though the space is open to invention.", "Actors can spin, dance, play music or tell part of the story during the change.", "Garcia's dramatic loop is a break from the main action into the past, future or a fantasy.", "It begins and ends in the same frozen picture, signalled by a lighting or sound change."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The space and the audience", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia says you must fix the performance space early, because designs, the ground plan and the blocking all depend on it.", "The three common layouts differ in how the audience sits.", "If you can stage in the three, he says, you can stage anywhere.", "On a thrust, Garcia suggests watching rehearsals from a different side each night, to see what each part of the audience sees.", "In the round there are no backdrops, so the set must be suggested by small furniture and the floor."]}, {"title": "Designers, budget and the production meeting", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Garcia treats designers as collaborators and equal voices in the creation of the play's world.", "Before each design meeting, read the play, list what that designer needs, collect pictures, and tell them the budget.", "Costume fittings need actors who are respectful and on time, because the designer has spent hours on them.", "Transition music should fit the style and carry the audience from one scene's mood to the next.", "Lighting enhances the set, can hide errors, and can move the audience to a new place."]}, {"title": "Tech, dress rehearsal, opening and review", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Tech is where the designers' work joins the actors. Garcia says you have about two days to do with the designers what you did with the actors in five weeks.", "Everyone will be tired and stressed, so be a steady, positive presence.", "Tell the actors beforehand that you will not be giving notes during tech.", "Designers have spent hours on their work, so some changes will feel like rejection. Phrase them positively, but be clear about what the production needs.", "The final dress is your last chance to guide the show. After that the actors must take command."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Direct a three-minute scene on stage and on camera, and review both", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the plan", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Choose a short scene of two or three characters and about three minutes, either from a public-domain play or one you have written yourself. Garcia notes that public-domain and new plays carry no rights fees.", "Garcia says new or public-domain plays are free to produce with no rights fee."]}, {"title": "Rehearse, stage and shoot", "chapters": ["Directing (Garcia), open textbook, chapters 1 to 9"], "points": ["Use Garcia's rehearsal order in miniature: a read-through, script work on each French scene, blocking, a stumble-through, scene work and a run.", "Warm up the group first, as Garcia recommends, with stretching and a focus game.", "Keep notes in praise-correct-praise form.", "Garcia lists warm-up exercises for blood flow, focus and collaboration.", "It follows Garcia's order in miniature, then adds the screen version."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-gb", "status": "unknown", "name": "Harley Granville-Barker", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [1], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Harley Granville-Barker", "chapters": ["Harley Granville-Barker"], "points": ["Britannica gives his dates and training.", "At the Savoy in 1912 he staged The Winter's Tale and Twelfth Night with continuous action on an open stage and quick, lightly stressed speech, extending an apron in front of the proscenium arch. The Winter's Tale ran with one fifteen-minute interval and the whole text in about three hours, with only six lines cut. Critics at first said the speed took the poetry out, and two months later accepted the same method in Twelfth Night."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "gb-prefaces", "status": "unknown", "name": "Prefaces to Shakespeare, volume VI, with an introduction by Edward M. Moore", "by": "Harley Granville-Barker, B. T. Batsford, 1974", "modules": [1], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Harley Granville-Barker", "chapters": ["Prefaces to Shakespeare, volume VI, with an introduction by Edward M. Moore"], "points": ["From Moore's introduction: he held that an interval is a decision, because it relaxes attention and lets time seem to pass; he cut six lines as unintelligible but kept a speech whose obscurity he judged intended; and he wanted a simple setting, a shell for the director's ideas.", "Moore says he extended an apron out in front of the proscenium to approximate a platform stage.", "He cut six lines as unclear but kept an obscure speech as a deliberate effect."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "gb-companion", "status": "unknown", "name": "Shakespeare's Dramatic Art, in A Companion to Shakespeare Studies, edited by H. Granville-Barker and G. B. Harrison", "by": "Harley Granville-Barker, Cambridge University Press, Internet Archive (Digital Library of India scan)", "modules": [1], "note": "1934; his own essay, pages 45 to 88, read from an OCR scan with some garbling", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Reading the play and finding your idea", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Harley Granville-Barker", "chapters": ["Shakespeare's Dramatic Art, in A Companion to Shakespeare Studies, edited by H. Granville-Barker and G. B. Harrison (1934; his own essay, pages 45 to 88, read from an OCR scan with some garbling)"], "points": ["We read his 1934 essay Shakespeare's Dramatic Art in full from an OCR scan with some garbling, and passages of Edward Moore's introduction to volume VI of the Prefaces that describe his 1912 Savoy productions. We did not read the Prefaces themselves.", "Treat time as a dramatic tool. He writes that for Shakespeare time has its dramatic uses but no rights of its own, and that he moves his play now quickly, now slowly, as he needs.", "Look for the relay. In Richard II, he says, from Bolingbroke's banishment on there is no sense of pause, and as each scene ends the thread is picked up in the next, like the running of a relay race.", "Count the devices for pace. In Hamlet, a play about delay, the speed of the action is kept up by fresh characters appearing, short scenes, swift-flowing verse, and changes between verse and prose.", "Let space be fluid. Shakespeare may move characters from one room to another in the middle of a scene, or Caesar from the street to the Senate House, and need not say where they are. The editors' scene localisations obscure this, and the student is advised to erase them."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "aap", "status": "full", "name": "An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood", "by": "Konstantin Stanislavski, Internet Archive (Digital Library of India scan)", "modules": [2, 3, "cap"], "note": "Text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "lessons": [{"title": "Given circumstances and the magic 'if'", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["Stanislavski's teacher character says the actor needs two things: a question that sets the imagination moving, and a world to answer it in.", "The question is the 'if', and the world is the given circumstances.", "The 'if' gives the push to a dormant imagination, while the given circumstances build the base the 'if' stands on.", "In An Actor Prepares the given circumstances mean the story of the play, its facts, period, place and conditions of life.", "They also include the director's reading and the staging, the sets, costumes, props, lighting and sound."]}, {"title": "Objectives, units and verbs", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["Stanislavski's teacher says a play is too big to swallow whole, so you carve it.", "He uses a roast turkey as the picture: cut it into large pieces, then smaller ones, and serve each to an actor.", "The pieces are called units, and the large ones form what he calls a channel, as buoys mark a channel for a pilot.", "The book warns against dividing a play into hundreds of tiny steps, because the actor then loses the whole.", "Divide for preparation, then let the pieces fuse into large units during performance."]}, {"title": "Screen lens: acting for the camera", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["Ask for the objective, not the face. The camera will find the thought if the actor is busy with an action.", "The objective and given circumstances stay. Only the size changes."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Konstantin Stanislavski", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["We read An Actor Prepares in full text, including the chapters on action, units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective. It is a teaching narrative with an invented class and a teacher called Tortsov, so we treat it as his method put into story form, not as a record of real lessons.", "Offer a supposition, not a lie. The teacher asks the class what they would do if a madman were behind a door. They measure the distance to the door, look for escape routes, barricade it with furniture.", "Explain why it works. The teacher points out that by using the word 'if' he frankly recognised he was offering only a supposition, and that nobody was forced to believe a lie. Everything is 'clear, honest and above-board'. The secret of 'if' lies in the fact that it does not use fear or force.", "Make 'if' a lever. He says 'if' acts as a lever to lift us out of the world of actuality into the realm of imagination, and that in moments of doubt, when thoughts, feelings and imagination are silent, remember 'if'.", "Give circumstances, then ask for action. For the housewarming exercise he supplies the supposed facts: an inheritance, a party, a cold flat, green wood that will not burn, guests about to arrive. Then: 'let me see what you would do if my supposed facts were true.'"]}]}, {"module": 3, "title": "Working with actors II: communion, the through-line and the rehearsal room", "lessons": [{"title": "Communion and adaptation: playing to each other", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["Stanislavski's teacher asks a student with whom he is in communion at this moment.", "The student answers that he is with nobody, because nobody is looking at him.", "The teacher disagrees. We are always in some contact with something, even with our own thoughts or the room.", "The book makes a practical point from this. On stage, an exchange only happens when one actor gives something and the other takes it.", "Actors who play to the audience or to their own idea of the role break the exchange."]}, {"title": "The through-line and the super-objective", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["The small objectives only matter if they lead somewhere.", "The book calls the aim that gathers them the super-objective and the path through the play the through-line of action.", "In the author's words, all the minor objectives should flow into one main current.", "A detail that does not serve it will look wrong or superfluous.", "The wording of the super-objective changes the play."]}, {"title": "The rehearsal room: a week, a note, a breath", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["Give the objective, not the result: the book warns that aiming at the result produces forced, hammy acting.", "Never ask an actor to repeat a good moment by copying it. The book describes a class that copied an earlier success and lost its truth.", "Give the objective and never ask for a copy of last night."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Konstantin Stanislavski", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["We read An Actor Prepares in full text, including the chapters on the unbroken line and the super-objective. It is an invented teaching story, not an evidence-based study, and Tortsov's lessons are Stanislavski's method in story form.", "Begin with the problem. The teacher asks what each student would do after the first reading of a play: act, think into the part, go off and try to feel it, start from the suppositions, or divide the play into small units. He answers that you must read the play many times and follow the steps the author took.", "Explain the line. He shows that disconnected movements are not a dance, and that in every art we must have an unbroken line. When the line emerges as a whole, he says, creative work has begun.", "Allow for breaks. He concedes that in healthy people there must be interruptions, but during the breaks a person continues to exist, so a line of some sort continues. The normal line is one with necessary interruptions.", "Build it backwards. He asks a student what he did that day, and advises going backwards from the present, because it is easier. The student recalls changing clothes, before that fencing and gymnastics, a cigarette, a singing lesson, and waking up. The short lines are then run forwards and backwards to fix them."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Direct a three-minute scene on stage and on camera, and review both", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Konstantin Stanislavski", "chapters": ["An Actor Prepares, translated by Elizabeth Reynolds Hapgood (read in full text from a scan of the Eyre Methuen edition: the chapters on action and the 'if', units and objectives, the unbroken line and the super-objective)"], "points": ["We read the chapter on units and objectives in full text, including the nine tests, the three types of objective, and the passage on why an objective must be a verb. It is a teaching narrative, so the tests are Stanislavski's through the teacher.", "Aim it at the partner. Objectives must be on our side of the footlights, directed toward the other actors and not toward the spectators.", "Make it personal and analogous. It should be personal yet analogous to the character's, and creative and artistic.", "Make it real and truthful. It should be real, live and human, not dead, conventional or theatrical, and truthful so that the actor, the partner and the audience can believe in it.", "Make it attractive and clear. It should attract and move the actor, and be clear cut and typical of the role, with no vagueness."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-stan", "status": "unknown", "name": "Konstantin Stanislavsky", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica (Sonia Moore), including Lee Strasberg's essay on the Method", "modules": [2, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Working with actors I: given circumstances, the magic 'if' and objectives", "lessons": [{"title": "Given circumstances and the magic 'if'", "chapters": ["Konstantin Stanislavsky"], "points": ["Britannica adds that the American Method was Lee Strasberg's adaptation, which encouraged actors to use their own emotional memory.", "Stanislavski himself kept changing his approach for forty years, and in 1935 he began a final technique he called the method of physical actions."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Konstantin Stanislavski", "chapters": ["Konstantin Stanislavsky"], "points": ["Britannica supplies his dates and the Moscow Art Theatre's opening.", "Stanislavski co-founded the Moscow Art Theatre, which opened on 26/10/1898 (14/10/1898 in the old calendar). Britannica says he was disappointed that first night because the acting only imitated outward gestures, and that he spent forty years developing what became the system. He would repeat a blunt test in rehearsal: he did not believe the actor.", "Britannica notes that the American Method's use of personal emotional memory is controversial. Never push a performer to relive a painful memory in rehearsal. Our advice: use imagined 'if' questions and actions instead, and stop if someone is distressed."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "Tech, dress rehearsal, opening and review", "chapters": ["Konstantin Stanislavsky"], "points": ["Britannica records that Stanislavski kept using one blunt test in rehearsal: I do not believe you (unverified: the quoted phrase was not found in the Britannica page we checked)."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Direct a three-minute scene on stage and on camera, and review both", "lessons": [{"title": "Present, score and review", "chapters": ["Konstantin Stanislavsky"], "points": ["Show both versions to a small audience, and ask them what they believed. Britannica records Stanislavski's habit of saying he did not believe the actor when something was false."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Konstantin Stanislavski", "chapters": ["Konstantin Stanislavsky"], "points": ["Britannica records that he repeated the test 'I do not believe you' in rehearsal. In your review, watch the recording for moments that look copied from the rehearsal and not freshly lived."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-bio", "status": "unknown", "name": "biomechanics", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [4], "note": "Theater", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["biomechanics (theater)"], "points": ["Define the system. Britannica describes biomechanics as an anti-realistic system of production developed in the early 1920s, drawing on commedia dell'arte, kabuki and the writings of Edward Gordon Craig.", "See how the actors were trained. They were coached as gymnasts and acrobats and emphasised movement and pantomime over words; the stage was exposed to the back wall and furnished with scaffolding, ladders and ramps.", "The actors were coached as gymnasts and acrobats and emphasised pantomime over words.", "The body can find a choice that the thought misses."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-biomech", "status": "unknown", "name": "Biomechanics", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [4], "note": "Meyerhold) (secondary", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["Biomechanics (Meyerhold) (secondary)"], "points": ["Note what the opposition was. Wikipedia says he felt Stanislavski's system overemphasised spirit and psychologising, while biomechanics drew on elementary laws of reflexes, and that constructivist costumes were simplified so mistakes stayed visible."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "tdr-gordon", "status": "part", "name": "Meyerhold's Biomechanics, The Drama Review 18", "by": "Mel Gordon, Cambridge University Press", "modules": [4], "note": "3), 1974 (only the opening extract on the publisher's page was available", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["Meyerhold's Biomechanics, The Drama Review 18(3), 1974 (only the opening extract on the publisher's page was available)"], "points": ["Read his own remark. In the extract from Gordon's article Meyerhold says he could determine twelve or thirteen rules for the training of an actor, but when polished perhaps no more than eight would remain. Gordon adds that he put his Biomechanics class on the same level as other movement courses such as acrobatics, modern dance and eurhythmics.", "He said he determined twelve or thirteen rules and would leave perhaps no more than eight."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "enc-mey", "status": "unknown", "name": "Vsevolod Meyerhold", "by": "Encyclopedia of World Biography, Encyclopedia.com", "modules": [4], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["Vsevolod Meyerhold"], "points": ["Do not teach acrobatics or risky movement without a qualified teacher, and keep falls and lifts out of the capstone. The sources we read describe gymnastic training, not how to do it safely. His theatre was closed on 08/01/1938 and he was arrested in 1939, so his later work was cut short."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "mey-site", "status": "unknown", "name": "Vsevolod Meyerhold: Biomechanics", "by": "Student study site on Google Sites, citing Thompson (2007); not verified against the book", "modules": [4], "note": "A brief introduction", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["Vsevolod Meyerhold: Biomechanics (a brief introduction)"], "points": ["Try the three-part action from the student site, with care. The site describes an actor exploring the economical gesture, the biggest gesture, then scaling down, with three parts to an action, called otkaz, posyl and tochka. It is a student summary of a book we have not checked.", "If the score is one bar long, the actor repeats it 15 times. Vary one thing each pass, such as the size: small, large, small again, as the student site describes."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-mey", "status": "unknown", "name": "Vsevolod Yemilyevich Meyerhold", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [4], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "Staging the scene: focus, distance, crosses and the frame", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Vsevolod Meyerhold", "chapters": ["Vsevolod Yemilyevich Meyerhold"], "points": ["We read Britannica's biography and its entry on biomechanics, Encyclopedia.com, a Wikipedia article, a student study site and the opening extract of Mel Gordon's 1974 article in The Drama Review. We did not read Meyerhold's own writings or the 1969 collection Meyerhold on Theatre.", "Place him. Meyerhold (1874 to 1940) studied at the Moscow Philharmonic school with Vladimir Nemirovich-Danchenko and joined the Moscow Art Theatre in 1898. He played Treplev in The Seagull, and left Moscow after four years. Encyclopedia.com says he later rejected Stanislavski's psychological realism, though they remained friends.", "Britannica's two entries do not agree on dates: the biography links the puppet-like staging to his 1906 Hedda Gabler, and the entry on biomechanics says it developed in the early 1920s. We treat the 1920s as the system's fuller form and 1906 as an early experiment. This is our inference.", "The first two columns follow the sources. The last column is our adaptation.", "He joined in 1898, two years after starting at the school in 1896."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "media-eis", "status": "unknown", "name": "Sergei Eisenstein and Five Methods of Montage", "by": "media-studies.com (a secondary account quoting Film Form)", "modules": [5], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Sergei Eisenstein", "chapters": ["Sergei Eisenstein and Five Methods of Montage"], "points": ["Define montage as collision. A secondary account says he saw montage as an idea arising from the collision of two independent shots, not from shots stuck together, and Wikipedia reports that he called montage 'the nerve of cinema' and contrasts it with Pudovkin's building by addition.", "A cut can make an idea from the collision of two shots."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-eis", "status": "unknown", "name": "Sergey Eisenstein", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [5], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Sergei Eisenstein", "chapters": ["Sergey Eisenstein"], "points": ["We read Britannica's biography, the film textbook's discussion of the Odessa Steps, a study guide on Eisenstein and a Wikipedia article on Soviet montage theory. We did not read Film Form or Eisenstein's essays, which are in copyright and were not available.", "Place him. Eisenstein (1898 to 1948) was born in Riga and entered the Proletkult Theatre in Moscow in 1920 as an assistant decorator. He developed an interest in kabuki, and made a short film for a stage production in 1923.", "Start from attractions. His first article on editing proposed a montage of attractions, in which chosen images are presented for maximum psychological impact. Wikipedia dates the essays to 1923 and 1924 and describes an attraction as an element that reliably affects an audience's attention and emotions."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-montage", "status": "unknown", "name": "Soviet montage theory", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [5], "note": "The five methods, the montage of attractions and the differences from Kuleshov and Pudovkin; secondary", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Shooting and cutting the scene: shots, coverage and rhythm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Sergei Eisenstein", "chapters": ["Soviet montage theory (the five methods, the montage of attractions and the differences from Kuleshov and Pudovkin; secondary)"], "points": ["Cut by length. In metric montage shots are cut to a set number of frames regardless of content, like a beat; Wikipedia says he favoured simple ratios such as 3:4, 2:4 and 1:4, and cites October.", "Cut by movement, then by tone. Rhythmic montage lets the movement inside the shot matter as much as length, with the Odessa Steps as the example; tonal montage uses the emotional meaning of shots, as after the death of the sailor Vakulinchuk in Potemkin.", "Cut for an idea. Overtonal montage combines the earlier three, and intellectual montage juxtaposes a few rich images to create a concept in no single shot: in Strike, workers being attacked are cut with a bull being slaughtered. He drew on Chinese and Japanese characters, where eye and water together mean crying.", "Wikipedia notes that Kuleshov's famous experiment, the same neutral face set beside soup, a coffin and a woman, has been challenged by later scholars as unscientific.", "The sources define the higher methods differently. Wikipedia says overtonal combines metric, rhythmic and tonal; a study guide speaks of the collective calculation of all the piece's appeals; another account calls it resonance beyond a single tone. We use the simplest reading and mark it as ours."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-brook", "status": "part", "name": "Peter Brook", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [6], "note": "Secondary; the first 100,000 characters read", "uses": [{"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Peter Brook", "chapters": ["Peter Brook (secondary; the first 100,000 characters read)"], "points": ["Wikipedia reports that the Mahabharata (first performed 1985) was acclaimed, and that post-colonial scholars criticised its claim to universalism. Treat the claim of a universal theatre with that criticism in mind."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-brook", "status": "unknown", "name": "Peter Brook", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Peter Brook", "chapters": ["Peter Brook"], "points": ["We read Britannica's biography, the first 100,000 characters of a Wikipedia biography, a Wikipedia entry on The Empty Space and the RSC's page on the 1970 Dream. We did not read The Empty Space, which is in copyright.", "Place him. Brook (1925 to 2022) directed his first Shakespeare in 1945 and became known for Marat/Sade (1964) and The Mahabharata (1985). In 1968 he published The Empty Space."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rsc-dream", "status": "unknown", "name": "Peter Brook 1970 production, A Midsummer Night's Dream", "by": "Royal Shakespeare Company", "modules": [6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Peter Brook", "chapters": ["Peter Brook 1970 production, A Midsummer Night's Dream"], "points": ["Strip the set. In 1970 Brook staged A Midsummer Night's Dream at the RSC. The RSC says the designer Sally Jacobs built a brilliantly lit white box in place of the usual pretty forest.", "Replace scenery with skill. The actors wore bright silks and performed circus skills such as plate-spinning and trapeze-swinging.", "Find one idea. Oberon and Theseus were played by one actor, and Titania and Hippolyta by one, to suggest the wood was the eruption of buried fears and desires.", "Know the lineage and the later road. The RSC adds that Brook took up the baton from Granville-Barker, who staged the play with minimal scenery almost sixty years earlier. Wikipedia says Brook won a Tony for Best Direction of a Play for the production, set up the International Centre for Theatre Research with Micheline Rozan in 1971, and has been based at the Bouffes du Nord in Paris since 1974.", "The 1970 Dream is described here from the RSC's and Wikipedia's pages, not from reviews or the production's own papers. The stripping test is our design, built from the empty-space idea and the staging."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-empty", "status": "unknown", "name": "The Empty Space", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 6, "title": "Space, audience, designers and the finished work", "lessons": [{"title": "The space and the audience", "chapters": ["The Empty Space"], "points": ["Peter Brook defined the empty space as any space in which theatre takes place.", "The idea is that a performer crossing a bare space while someone watches is enough for theatre."]}, {"title": "The lecturer's method: Peter Brook", "chapters": ["The Empty Space"], "points": ["Know the book's frame. It came from four lectures and examines four modes: Deadly, Holy, Rough and Immediate. Brook defined an empty space as any space in which theatre takes place. We have not read it, so use the names as a guide to what to look up."]}]}], "origin": ""}], "sections": [{"title": "Book supplied by the learner (added 19/10/2026)", "items": ["**The Art of Alfred Hitchcock, Donald Spoto, second revised edition, Anchor Books, 1992 (Knopf Doubleday ebook, 2012)** (46 chapters, about 96,000 words). The review is written up in `source-review-hitchcock.md`.", "Read in full: Chapter 20 Rope, Chapter 23 Strangers on a Train, Chapter 26 Rear Window, Chapter 33 Psycho, and the introduction to the centenary edition.", "Read in full on 19/10/2026 (45 of 46 parts; only the contents list was skipped). Review: `source-review-hitchcock.md`, Status: complete.", "Added to the course: planting, bookends and the controlling image (module 1), evidence from cast and crew on directing actors (module 2), the cost of long takes and lost preparation (module 3), and a worked model for designing a sequence (module 5). Spoto agrees with the earlier long-take case study, so nothing there was removed.", "Limits: Spoto is one critic's reading, and several points rest on Hitchcock's remarks as Spoto reports them. The accounts of Hitchcock and actors come from a critic and one leading actor, not from rehearsal records. Facts about the films (running of takes, camera tricks) are Spoto's account and were not checked against other sources."]}], "counts": {"full": 6, "part": 2, "search": 0, "none": 0, "unknown": 17}, "hasMd": true};
